{"id":"mu-earnings-fq4-fy2026","title":"MU earnings — Micron, fiscal Q4 FY2026","section":"Earnings","date":"2026-09-30","html":"<p>Management sees memory staying short for at least two more years with most of next year already sold, and the thing that could spoil it is the price cycle turning once the gains slow and new supply arrives.</p>\n<p>Reported 2026-09-30. Next report: not found (the quarter ends around Nov 30). Updated 2026-11-12. Research, not investment advice.</p>\n<p><strong>This report is six weeks old. The next quarter closes in about three weeks.</strong></p>\n<h3 id=\"the-short-version\">The short version</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Demand:</strong> stronger. More than 75% of fiscal 2027 shipments are already committed, and customers have put up $32B, mostly as cash deposits.</li><li><strong>Guidance:</strong> raised. Fiscal Q1 revenue $61.5B, plus or minus $1.5B, against a guide of $50.0B for the quarter just reported. Gross margin about 86.25%, a touch under the 87.0% just posted.</li><li><strong>Biggest worry:</strong> the memory price cycle turning. Price gains are already slowing, and PC and phone units could fall by double digits.</li><li><strong>Chart:</strong> no lean. Price is above the 200-day ($761.89) but stalled at my 38.2% line ($825.17). A close above $825.17 leans up; a close below $761.89 leans down.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"the-numbers\">The numbers</h3>\n<ul><li>Revenue $54.23B against $11.32B a year ago, and against an estimate of $51.51B. Fiscal 2026 revenue $133.19B.</li><li>GAAP EPS $32.87 against a $31.94 estimate; non-GAAP EPS $33.42. The estimate is from Webull and analysts usually estimate the adjusted figure, so that EPS comparison mixes GAAP and adjusted. Treat it as rough.</li><li>Non-GAAP gross margin 87.0%. Revenue beat the top of the guided range ($50.0B, plus or minus $1.0B).</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"what-management-said-about-the-road-ahead\">What management said about the road ahead</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Next quarter:</strong> revenue $61.5B, plus or minus $1.5B; non-GAAP gross margin about 86.25%; non-GAAP EPS $38.15, plus or minus $1.00.</li><li><strong>Fiscal 2027:</strong> no revenue figure. Revenue is expected to grow every quarter, with Q1 as the low point for gross margin. &quot;Micron delivered record fiscal 2026 results, and we expect an even stronger fiscal 2027.&quot; Sanjay Mehrotra, Chairman and CEO.</li><li><strong>Supply:</strong> much tighter in 2027 and 2028 than in 2026. Management says it cannot see when DRAM supply and demand balance. Cleanroom space is the main limit on output.</li><li><strong>Contracts:</strong> 26 long-term customer agreements, estimated at over 35% of revenue through 2030, mostly with floor and ceiling prices.</li><li><strong>AI memory:</strong> agreements cover the vast majority of 2027 supply of high-bandwidth memory, at significantly higher prices.</li><li><strong>Pricing:</strong> increases are expected to be more moderate after Q1.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"what-management-is-worried-about\">What management is worried about</h3>\n<ol><li><strong>Prices losing speed.</strong> Management itself expects more moderate increases after Q1, and the margin guide (about 86.25%) is already a little under the 87.0% just reported.</li><li><strong>PCs and phones.</strong> Unit sales could fall by double digits as memory makes those devices dearer.</li><li><strong>Returns on new capacity.</strong> Capital spending is about $25B in the first half of fiscal 2027 and higher in the second half, with start-up costs for plants that come online between 2027 and 2030. The 10-K lists returns on new capacity as a risk.</li><li><strong>Few customers.</strong> About half of revenue comes from the top ten.</li><li><strong>China, tariffs and export controls,</strong> plus conditions attached to government incentives for the new plants.</li></ol>\n<h3 id=\"the-big-worry-the-memory-price-cycle-turning-both-ways\">The big worry: the memory price cycle turning, both ways</h3>\n<p><strong>What would ease it</strong></p>\n<ul><li>The contracts hold. Floor prices on 26 long-term agreements and $32B of customer commitments mean a softer market does not reach Micron all at once.</li><li>AI memory stays sold out. The vast majority of 2027 high-bandwidth memory is already placed at significantly higher prices; a TrendForce headline puts the 2027 blended price for it up 121% from 2026.</li><li>New supply stays slow. Micron's own new plants arrive between 2027 and 2030, and TrendForce reports China's CXMT is not expected to fully commercialize its next memory design until after 2028.</li><li>The chip buyers keep building. AMD raised its 2027 growth view to more than 80%, and Nvidia's supply commitments, mostly memory, rose to $279B.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>What would make it worse</strong></p>\n<ul><li>The ceilings bite. The same contracts that set floors also cap what Micron can charge.</li><li>Consumer demand breaks. AMD expects PC units to fall by a double-digit percentage in 2027, Dell says cost-sensitive customers are delaying PC upgrades, and TrendForce says phone production is weakening and notebook inventories are rising.</li><li>AI chips use less memory each. TrendForce notes that if Nvidia moves a coming chip to shorter memory stacks, memory per chip could fall about 33%.</li><li>AI spending slows. The Fed now calls the AI buildout an inflation source and futures put about 90% odds on a December rate hike; one governor flagged more than $800B of AI debt. TSMC already calls its 2027 planning prudent.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>How this company is placed against it</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Balance sheet: cash and investments of $73.5B against $5.2B of debt. Micron can fund the build-out without borrowing, which matters with rates rising.</li><li>Contracts: over 35% of revenue through 2030 under agreement, more than 75% of fiscal 2027 shipments committed, and deposits in hand. That is far more cover than in past memory cycles, by management's own description of the agreements.</li><li>Exposure: it is still spending about $25B in half a year on capacity that arrives from 2027 on. If prices turn before then, the plants land in a weaker market. How Samsung and SK Hynix are placed: not found.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"money-plans\">Money plans</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Capital spending:</strong> $10.8B in the quarter, about $11.5B next quarter, about $25B in the first half of fiscal 2027 and higher in the second half. No full-year figure.</li><li><strong>Buybacks:</strong> authorization raised to $35.16B; repurchases can start Dec 9, 2026.</li><li><strong>Dividend:</strong> $0.15 a quarter.</li><li><strong>Debt:</strong> $5.2B against $73.5B of cash and investments.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"people-and-governance\">People and governance</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>CEO:</strong> Sanjay Mehrotra, 68, CEO since May 2017 and Chairman since January 2025. Not a Micron founder.</li><li><strong>CFO:</strong> Mark Murphy, 59, CFO since April 2022.</li><li><strong>Successor named:</strong> no one is named. In August 2026 the board created two president roles: Manish Bhatia (54), President and Chief Operating Officer, and Scott DeBoer (60), President and Chief Technology and Products Officer. The release did not call either a successor. That they are the bench is my reading, not the company's.</li><li><strong>Chair/CEO split:</strong> no. Mehrotra holds both. Lead independent director: not found.</li><li><strong>Control:</strong> one class of common stock. No holder above 10% was identified in the part of the 10-K read.</li><li><strong>Recent changes or investigations:</strong> Sumit Sadana, the longtime Chief Business Officer, moved to Senior Advisor to the CEO in the August reshuffle. A stockholder proposal to let 10% holders call a special meeting was on the January 2026 ballot with the board against it; the result was not found. The legal proceedings section of the 10-K was not read, so investigations: not found.</li><li><strong>How much rests on one person:</strong> less than at a founder-run company, and less than a few months ago. Mehrotra is 68 and holds both top seats, which makes succession a live question, but two presidents now run operations and technology under him and the CFO has been in place over four years.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"does-the-chart-agree\">Does the chart agree?</h3>\n<figure class=\"chart\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" data-ticker=\"MU\" viewBox=\"0 0 760 400\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"MU · daily · my levels (not yours) chart\" font-family=\"Arial, sans-serif\"><text x=\"8\" y=\"18\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#20242C\" class=\"c-ink\">MU · daily · my levels (not yours)</text><line x1=\"8\" x2=\"610\" y1=\"374.0\" y2=\"374.0\" stroke=\"#E3DACB\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-grid\"/><line x1=\"8\" x2=\"610\" y1=\"289.0\" y2=\"289.0\" stroke=\"#E3DACB\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-grid\"/><line x1=\"8\" x2=\"610\" y1=\"204.0\" y2=\"204.0\" stroke=\"#E3DACB\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-grid\"/><line x1=\"8\" x2=\"610\" y1=\"119.0\" y2=\"119.0\" stroke=\"#E3DACB\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-grid\"/><line x1=\"8\" x2=\"610\" y1=\"34.0\" y2=\"34.0\" stroke=\"#E3DACB\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-grid\"/><line x1=\"10.3\" x2=\"10.3\" y1=\"198.6\" y2=\"222.8\" stroke=\"#2F6E4A\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-up\"/><rect x=\"8.9\" y=\"205.7\" width=\"2.9\" height=\"5.7\" fill=\"#2F6E4A\" class=\"c-up\"/><line x1=\"14.9\" x2=\"14.9\" y1=\"185.0\" y2=\"255.1\" stroke=\"#A63A2E\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-dn\"/><rect x=\"13.5\" y=\"185.5\" width=\"2.9\" height=\"27.2\" fill=\"#A63A2E\" class=\"c-dn\"/><line x1=\"19.6\" x2=\"19.6\" y1=\"201.5\" y2=\"240.1\" stroke=\"#A63A2E\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-dn\"/><rect x=\"18.1\" y=\"228.7\" width=\"2.9\" height=\"6.9\" fill=\"#A63A2E\" class=\"c-dn\"/><line x1=\"24.2\" x2=\"24.2\" y1=\"181.0\" y2=\"233.7\" stroke=\"#2F6E4A\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-up\"/><rect x=\"22.8\" y=\"181.5\" width=\"2.9\" height=\"